<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/expert/computability-and-complexity">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Computability and complexity theory ask the two deepest questions in computer science:
    <strong>what can be computed at all?</strong> and <strong>what can be computed
    efficiently?</strong> The answers are surprising: some problems are provably impossible for
    any computer, and an enormous class of practical problems sits in an unresolved middle —
    checkable fast, possibly never findable fast. What makes this subject sing is that both
    extremes grow out of ordinary algorithm design. The craft of designing a solution, testing it
    with trace tables and amending it is the same craft that, followed far enough, walks into
    undecidability and the P versus NP question. This chapter walks that road: first the
    practitioner's method, then the theory that bounds it — because an engineer who knows where
    the walls are stops wasting months trying to build through them.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from Turing's tape to the million-dollar question" widget="equationStep" config={comp2Cfg} />

  <h2>Designing and creating a solution</h2>
  <p>
    Serious algorithm design starts before code, with a disciplined sequence. <strong>Decompose</strong>
    the problem ("produce a merit list" becomes: read the file → compute each student's average →
    sort descending → output). <strong>Identify the inputs, outputs and preconditions</strong>
    (how many marks? what if a file is empty or a mark is negative?). <strong>Choose
    representations</strong>, because the representation often decides the algorithm: marks as
    parallel arrays allow index-based lookup; a single list of records allows stable sorting.
    <strong>Write the algorithm</strong> as pseudocode — structured English with the precision of
    the three constructs sequence, selection and iteration — and only then translate. The reason
    this stage is worth its cost: an error in the design costs minutes to fix; the same error
    discovered in tested code costs a debugging session, and in shipped software a release cycle.
    A worked scale: Google's interview canonical problem "design a spell-checker" is not a coding
    task at all — it is this discipline (what counts as a word? what latency? what happens to
    memory when the dictionary is 500,000 entries?) and candidates who skip it fail predictably.
  </p>

  <h2>Standard methods of solution</h2>
  <p>
    Most working algorithms are compositions of a dozen standard patterns, and knowing them is
    knowing what to reach for. <strong>Counting, totalling, max/min</strong>: initialise, walk the
    array, update — the loop behind every dashboard. <strong>Searching</strong>: linear search
    checks items one by one, averaging n/2 comparisons (500,000 for a million items); binary
    search on sorted data halves the range each step, needing only about 20 comparisons for the
    same million — a 25,000-fold difference that is the whole argument for keeping data sorted.
    <strong>Sorting</strong>: the naive bubble sort does about n²/2 comparisons (500 billion for
    a million items — hours), while merge sort does n log₂n (~20 million — under a second),
    which is why libraries ship merge or quick sort and nobody ships bubble. Each pattern has a
    correctness argument you can state in a sentence (binary search works because the loop
    invariant "target is inside the current range" is maintained), and composability is the point:
    hash a key, chain for collisions, sort the buckets — real structures are these atoms joined.
  </p>

  <h2>Test data, trace tables and debugging</h2>
  <p>
    Testing has a taxonomy, and the exam-correct one is worth having exactly. <strong>Normal
    data</strong> (values inside the expected range), <strong>boundary data</strong> (the exact
    edges: 0 and 100 for a percentage, the first and last record of a file), <strong>erroneous
    data</strong> (negative marks, "abc" where a number belongs), and for loops the critical
    <strong>extremes of iteration</strong> (zero times, once, many). The mechanism that makes a
    loop trustworthy is the <strong>trace table</strong>: a column per variable, one row per
    iteration, recording every change. Trace this loop for a list of three marks 60, 40, 80:
    total starts 0, becomes 60, 100, 180; count runs 1, 2, 3; average printed as 180/3 = 60.
    Two rows reveal the classic bug instantly: if the average is computed <em>inside</em> the loop
    it prints three partial averages — the trace table shows it in five seconds that code staring
    would not. Trace tables are also the mental model behind professional practice: assertions,
    unit tests and formal verification are all automated trace-table thinking, asking "what are
    the variables after iteration k?" Debugging itself is the runtime version: reproduce, isolate
    (halve the input until it breaks), instrument (breakpoints and watches), form a hypothesis,
    and fix the cause — never the symptom.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "if all my tests pass, the program is correct." Dijkstra's
    line is still the expert position: testing shows the presence of bugs, never their absence.
    Tests sample the input space; correctness is a claim about all of it. This is not pedantry —
    it is literally true, and the halting problem below is the reason no tool can ever close the
    gap completely.
  </p>

  <h2>Writing and amending algorithms</h2>
  <p>
    Algorithms are written to be read, and amended more often than they are written — maintenance
    is most of software's lifetime cost. The craft rules follow: meaningful identifiers
    (<code>totalMarks</code> beats <code>t</code>), one purpose per subprogram, parameters passed
    in rather than globals reached for, and a loop invariant you could state aloud. Amending is a
    discipline of its own: re-read the requirement, trace the affected path through the existing
    algorithm, make the smallest change that restores the contract, then re-run the whole test
    suite — because the amendment that fixes the average for one class and breaks the empty-file
    case is the amendment that ships. Worked example: an algorithm that prints each student's
    percentage is asked to weight coursework 40% and exam 60%. The naive amendment multiplies and
    adds inside the print statement; the correct one changes the data structure to carry both
    marks and computes <code>0.4 × coursework + 0.6 × exam</code> once — and the trace table
    through both versions shows why the second survives the next amendment (weightings change)
    and the first does not.
  </p>

  <h2>The halting problem: some questions have no algorithm</h2>
  <p>
    In 1936, before any computer existed, Turing defined computation itself: a machine with a
    tape, a read-write head and a finite rule table — and proved that this absurdly simple device
    computes everything every later "more powerful" proposal could (the Church–Turing thesis: no
    effective procedure has ever escaped it). Then he proved its shadow side. Question: can a
    program H decide, for any program P and input I, whether P halts on I? Turing showed not, by
    paradox: assume H exists; build a program D that, fed any program P, does the opposite of
    what H predicts about P fed to itself; now run D on D. H must call D's behaviour correctly
    and wrongly at once — contradiction. No cleverness escapes it: the set of halting programs is
    not decidable. The consequence is practical and permanent: no tool can ever detect all
    viruses, prove all programs bug-free, or optimise away all dead code, because each reduces to
    halting. Vendors claiming 100% guarantees are selling past a theorem.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the halting problem means we can never tell whether any
    program halts." Not so. Specific programs are routinely proven to halt (a bounded loop is
    trivial), and industrial tools verify real systems. What is impossible is a <em>single
    procedure that decides every case</em>. The wall is about the general method, not the
    individual instance — the distinction between a theorem about the set and a fact about a
    member is the whole subtlety.
  </p>

  <h2>Complexity: measuring how hard "hard" is</h2>
  <p>
    Computability asks <em>whether</em>; complexity asks <em>at what cost</em>, measured as growth
    of running time with input size n. <strong>P</strong> is the class solvable in polynomial time
    — sorting (n log n), shortest paths, matrix multiplication — where doubling the input multiplies
    the work by a civilised factor. <strong>NP</strong> is the class whose <em>proposed answers
    verify</em> in polynomial time: a filled sudoku, a timetable, a route under 1,000 km. The
    asymmetry is the drama: for 3-SAT with n variables there are 2<sup>n</sup> assignments to
    search (n = 300 is more than atoms in the observable universe), yet any assignment is checked
    in milliseconds. Cook (1971) and Levin (1973) proved SAT is <strong>NP-complete</strong> —
    every NP problem reduces to it — and thousands of problems from chip layout to protein
    folding followed by the same reduction argument: solve one fast and you solve them all. The
    pragmatic expert position: assume finding is exponential (most complexity theorists believe
    P is not NP), and engineer around it — Concorde, the travelling-salesman code, found the
    <em>exact</em> optimum for an 85,900-city instance in 2006 by exploiting structure no worst-case
    analysis sees, while 2-approximation algorithms and integer programming handle the rest of
    industry daily.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "NP-hard means unsolvable." NP-hardness is a statement about
    <em>worst-case scaling</em>, not about any particular instance: your 20-city delivery route is
    solved exactly by brute force (20!/2 is about 10<sup>18</sup> — too big; but branch-and-bound
    finishes in milliseconds), and real timetables carry structure that makes them tractable.
    Equally wrong: "NP" as "non-polynomial". It means <em>nondeterministic polynomial</em> —
    checkable fast — and P could still equal NP; that is precisely the unresolved
    million-dollar question.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Beyond P and NP.</strong> The complexity zoo extends upward: PSPACE (games like
    generalised Go and chess are complete here — deciding whether a position is a win may take
    exponential time even to check), EXPTIME, and the undecidable above all of them. Downward
    sits the recent gem of the IP=PSPACE era and, most usefully, <strong>BQP</strong>: the class
    quantum computers solve efficiently. Shor's algorithm puts factoring in BQP — believed outside
    P — which is why cryptography is already migrating to post-quantum schemes; complexity theory
    is the only field that sets the security budget of the internet.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The status of P versus NP.</strong> Fifty years of attack have produced barriers as
    well as progress: relativisation (Baker–Gill–Solovay, 1975), natural proofs (Razborov–Rudich,
    1997) and algebrisation each show that whole proof <em>styles</em> cannot resolve the
    question, which is why progress now comes from unexpected directions (Mulmuley's geometric
    complexity theory among them). The working consensus — P is not NP — carries a sober caveat
    worth repeating to every optimisation enthusiast: even if a problem is in P, the polynomial
    could be n<sup>100</sup>; and even where it is exponential, average cases and approximations
    can be easy. Theory sets the shape of the wall; engineering finds the doors in it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Verification as the frontier.</strong> Since full program correctness is undecidable,
    the field has industrialised partial answers: type systems and memory-safe languages
    eliminate bug classes entirely (checked in polynomial time); model checkers exhaustively
    verify finite-state designs (chip verification runs on this); proof assistants (Coq, Lean)
    have certified a C compiler (CompCert) and an operating-system kernel (seL4) — human-guided
    proofs dodging the undecidability wall by paying human effort instead of machine
    omniscience. The design-and-test craft from the start of this chapter returns at the end,
    upgraded: the trace table's question — what does this variable hold at iteration k — became
    the inductive hypothesis of a machine-checked proof.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Turing, "On Computable Numbers" (1936); Cook (1971), Levin (1973); Sipser, Introduction to the Theory of Computation.</p>
</TopicPage>
